WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
- Please welcome Rosario Dawson and Retta.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.966
(audience applauding)

00:00:06.966 --> 00:00:08.330
Oh, I'm so happy to see you.

00:00:10.173 --> 00:00:12.502
- It's so good to see you too.

00:00:12.502 --> 00:00:13.335
- Hi.

00:00:13.335 --> 00:00:15.850
- Rosario, I know you've moved East.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.950
Kickin' it in Newark with your boyfriend.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.490
- (laughs) Yeah, I'm a Jersey girl now.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:24.551
I didn't see that coming.

00:00:24.551 --> 00:00:25.384
- Welcome.

00:00:25.384 --> 00:00:26.730
(all laughing)

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.030
- How do you like it?
- Thanks.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:33.483
I'm learning a lot of different
Jersey factoids to be sure.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.630
Jersey the home of saltwater taffy.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:38.463
- Retta.

00:00:38.463 --> 00:00:41.700
I'm so grateful because
you were kind enough

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.400
to do the pilot of this show.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.020
And we put on blindfolds

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.740
and like had fun and did taste tests

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:49.920
and just had a really good time.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:50.753
And it was such...

00:00:50.753 --> 00:00:53.960
You honored this show
with your presence and

00:00:55.119 --> 00:00:56.140
I owe you everything and

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.260
I want to work

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.899
alongside of you.
- You're welcome.

00:00:58.899 --> 00:00:59.732
- For life.

00:00:59.732 --> 00:01:00.565
And I just love you.

00:01:00.565 --> 00:01:01.398
And I thank you.

00:01:01.398 --> 00:01:04.080
And I'm so glad we're back
together here on this real show

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.990
and Rosario,

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.350
I have to tell you guys,

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:12.750
I am so blown away by your podcast.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.250
It's amazing what you guys have done.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.430
- This was kind of put together

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:17.780
by a bunch of different senators.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:21.080
Women created a whole space, you know,

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.150
create a whole fund

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.430
and a whole committee for
this to make it happen.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.120
So, you know, it was just about trying

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.410
to be as prepared as
possible for the Centennial.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.283
- I think the production was amazing.

00:01:32.795 --> 00:01:35.100
I really was like, "Dang,
am I part of that?"

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.490
Because I had no idea how
much was going into it.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.270
- I think that, you know,

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.020
you listen to a lot of podcasts

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.800
and some of it's sort of
really fun and meandering.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.300
Your guy's is incredibly focused.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.780
It's so educational.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.230
It's super interesting.

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.610
I was wrapped from the beginning.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:53.990
It hooked me

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:58.410
and I was just so excited
to understand this history.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.400
Why was it so important
for you guys to do this?

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.473
- For me,

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.810
one I was shocked

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.830
at how little I did know
about the suffrage movement

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:10.900
and anything that can encourage

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.230
more people to get registered
and get out to vote

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.030
and understand the importance of it.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.170
You know voting has

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.670
a direct relationship

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:24.570
to what's gonna go on at your job,

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.193
in your supermarket,

00:02:27.735 --> 00:02:30.730
in your towns, and your
restaurants, and all of that.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.437
That made me say, "Okay,

00:02:33.437 --> 00:02:34.827
"I don't like certain things.

00:02:34.827 --> 00:02:36.900
"I might want to have a say in it."

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.580
- And Rosario, you talked
about your grandmother

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.400
being your influence.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.233
- Yeah.

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:45.091
She was born and raised in Puerto Rico.

00:02:45.091 --> 00:02:48.670
And as I say in the podcast, you know,

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.490
she raised me with the understanding

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.250
that just because she spoke
with an accent didn't mean

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:54.670
that she would think with one and

00:02:57.129 --> 00:02:58.010
that she had a voice

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.440
even though she represented
a marginalized person

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.350
according to what she
represented on paper.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.270
She was a mother of five single mother,

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:12.270
her daughter had me out
of wedlock as a teenager.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.550
So we fell into the statistics

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:17.950
of people that so often are marginalized.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.000
And what I loved about
this podcast in particular

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.730
was that it was starting
to show the history

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.370
of how women had been voting long before

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.020
the 19th amendment was ratified.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.060
And that there were a
lot of women of color

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.600
who were still denied that
right even after it was.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.580
And I think this podcast helps

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.050
to elaborate just how many more people

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.760
are part of a history that has made

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.270
it possible for us to have the rights

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:42.460
that we have today

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.550
and that we have to
stop marginalizing them

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.240
and discounting them from the story.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.140
- And you said in the podcast

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:52.030
Rosario that you look to history
sometimes in the present.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.670
And that's something I do too

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.320
because I want to
understand where we've been

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.760
in order to be more educated
about where we're going.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.450
And I'm so honored to speak about it

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.110
on the show and you can go

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:08.050
to our website and get links to it.

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:10.710
And I just thank you guys so
much for coming on the show

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:11.720
and talking about it.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:12.930
Hey, before you guys go,

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.540
can you do us a favor?

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:16.700
We wanted to try something
and you'll be the first people

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.398
to ever do it with us.

00:04:18.398 --> 00:04:19.231
- Okay.

00:04:19.231 --> 00:04:21.674
- Our VFFs our virtual friends and family

00:04:21.674 --> 00:04:23.780
is called 15 seconds of Fame.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.900
And they wanted to ask you a question

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.960
in 15 seconds that you would
have to answer in 15 seconds.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:30.595
How do you feel about that?

00:04:30.595 --> 00:04:32.150
(woman snaps fingers)
- [Retta] Alright.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.040
- Okay, great.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:34.496
Oh, hi, Brant.

00:04:34.496 --> 00:04:35.330
(audience claps)

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.420
Brant, are you ready for your 15 seconds

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.530
of fame and your question?

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:40.986
- I am, Drew.

00:04:40.986 --> 00:04:41.819
Thank you.

00:04:41.819 --> 00:04:42.652
- Perfect.

00:04:42.652 --> 00:04:43.780
Rosario, you're going to answer first

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.070
and then I'll go to you Retta.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:46.733
And the clock starts now go.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.960
- Rosario, what was first ever job

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.410
before you became an actress?

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.240
- I was a babysitter and a tutor.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.666
Actually my very first job
was working on Sesame Street.

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:01.720
(audience cheers)

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:02.553
(bell dings)

00:05:02.553 --> 00:05:03.386
- Retta, your turn.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:09.120
- My first job was, I
was a cash cashier at a

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.240
Nickels which was like a Target (laughs).

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:14.564
- Perfect.

00:05:14.564 --> 00:05:15.397
(bell dings)

00:05:15.397 --> 00:05:16.230
(audience applauds)

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:17.660
- Okay I think that went okay.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:21.380
I'm excited and that was really fun.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:25.550
And I hope I get to get a
15-second answer because

00:05:26.795 --> 00:05:28.930
I would not do well in 15 seconds

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:30.340
'cause I never know when to shut up.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.126
So this was so fun.

00:05:32.126 --> 00:05:32.959
(woman chuckles)

00:05:32.959 --> 00:05:33.792
Thank you guys so much.

00:05:33.792 --> 00:05:35.760
I really appreciate you guys coming on.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:36.820
I love what you're doing

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.840
and we're here to shout
it from the rooftops.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.623
Thank you so much.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:45.780
Rosario and Retta's podcast is called

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.840
And Nothing Less and before we go to break

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.940
I want to show you a clip
from a documentary I worked on

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.440
years ago that we spoke about

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.690
that helped start my voting fire.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:57.070
Alright, take a look.

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:58.040
Can I ask a question?

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.130
Are you going to vote this year?

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:02.053
Why do we have the lowest
rate of young voters?

00:06:02.053 --> 00:06:03.875
- [Man] Give them a reason to vote.

00:06:03.875 --> 00:06:07.130
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.520
- I have also come to believe

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.780
that we all passionately want
to feel a part of something

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.940
so that we are not alone.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:18.600
And although though voting
is a very personal ritual

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.841
it brings us all together
in the most profound way.

00:06:22.841 --> 00:06:25.424
(upbeat music)

